// Yankee Doodle Lilac — Syringa vulgaris 'Yankee Doodle'
// Edit this file to update plant-specific content.

#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Yankee Doodle Lilac", "Syringa vulgaris 'Yankee Doodle'", "yankee_lilac")[
  Yankee Doodle is a common French hybrid lilac selected for its large, dark purple-red, very fragrant flower panicles. Larger and faster-growing than Miss Kim (10–12 ft. tall). Like all common lilacs, it blooms profusely when well sited but requires full sun and good drainage. Established 2+ years — on drip irrigation.
]

=== Watering

On drip irrigation with a *1 gph emitter*. See the Irrigation & Watering section for the shared drip schedule. Consistent moisture during the spring bloom period and early summer — when next year's flower buds are setting — is the most critical period for this plant. The shared schedule through June directly supports bloom quality the following year.

=== Fertilization

Per soil profile, use N-K formula only (16-0-8). Apply once in early spring before leaf-out. Supplement with potassium sulfate (0-0-50) in late summer or fall — potassium, not phosphorus, is what supports strong flower bud set and cold hardiness, and your K is LOW. Apply chelated micronutrients in spring. Never fertilize after mid-July.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Prune immediately after bloom — this is critical. The plant sets next year's flower buds right after this year's bloom ends (June). Pruning in fall, winter, or early spring removes those buds. Remove faded flower clusters just below the bloom. Every few years, remove the oldest, thickest canes at the base to rejuvenate and control size. Lilacs can tolerate hard rejuvenation pruning (cutting to 12–18" from the ground) if they have become overgrown — they will skip 1–2 years of bloom but return vigorously.

=== Wildlife Value

Dense, heavily scented flowers in May attract a wide variety of beneficial insects including large butterflies (swallowtails, admirals), bumble bees, and native solitary bees. Hummingbirds occasionally probe the flower clusters. The large, multi-stem structure offers substantial nesting cover for house finches and collared doves. In winter, the thick stems provide wind shelter for small birds. Cottontail rabbits commonly browse young shoots but rarely damage established plants significantly.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=common+lilac+powdery+mildew+treatment")[Powdery mildew] is prevalent on French hybrid lilacs in late summer — white powdery coating on leaves. Improve air circulation through pruning, avoid overhead watering, treat with neem oil if needed. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=lilac+borer+treatment")[Lilac borer] leaves sawdust at branch bases; prune and dispose of affected wood. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=oyster+shell+scale+lilac+treatment")[Oyster shell scale] appears as small armor-like bumps on bark — treat with dormant oil in early spring. In Colorado's dry climate, foliar diseases are less severe than in humid regions.

=== Other Care

Mulch 2–3 inches deep in the shrub bed. Leave 4 inches of bare soil around the base of each stem. Every spring, check the depth of the soil at the crown — lilac stems should never be buried. Remove any suckers that arise from the base if you want a multi-stem shrub; transplant or remove entirely. Deadhead spent blooms right after flowering to redirect energy to next year's buds.
